    "title": "pandas.PeriodIndex.freqstr — pandas 3.1.0.dev0+1386.gcb2086a1a4 documentation",
    "status": 200,
    "markdown": "# pandas.PeriodIndex.freqstr#\n\n- property PeriodIndex.freqstr[source]#\n- Return the frequency object as a string if it’s set, otherwise None. This property returns a string representation of the frequency (e.g., `'D'` for daily,`'h'` for hourly) when one has been set\non the index, either explicitly or via inference.See also \n  - `DatetimeIndex.inferred_freq`\n  - Returns a string representing a frequency generated by infer_freq.\n Examples For DatetimeIndex: >>> idx = pd.DatetimeIndex([\"1/1/2020 10:00:00+00:00\"], freq=\"D\") >>> idx.freqstr 'D' The frequency can be inferred if there are more than 2 points: >>> idx = pd.DatetimeIndex( ... [\"2018-01-01\", \"2018-01-03\", \"2018-01-05\"], freq=\"infer\" ... ) >>> idx.freqstr '2D' For PeriodIndex: >>> idx = pd.PeriodIndex([\"2023-1\", \"2023-2\", \"2023-3\"], freq=\"M\") >>> idx.freqstr 'M'",
    "markdown_length": 829,
